# PNG + c15   · insert BEFORE the line `                    <h3>How Metals and Non-Metals Differ Visually</h3>`
                    <figure>
                        <div style="position: relative; display: inline-block; max-width: 100%;">
                            <img src="images/lesson_11_15_metallic_comparison.png"
                             alt="Three red spheres rendered with the same base color and roughness but different Metallic values. The left sphere at Metallic 0.0 shows a clear diffuse red surface with a soft white highlight. The center sphere at Metallic 0.5 looks uncanny and physically wrong and is crossed out with a NEVER USE warning. The right sphere at Metallic 1.0 reads as true metal, with darker body and colored, environment-tinted reflections."
                             loading="lazy"
                             style="display: block; max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 0;">
                            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 1920 800" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet" style="position: absolute; inset: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; pointer-events: none;" font-family="system-ui, -apple-system, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, sans-serif" aria-labelledby="c15-title c15-desc">
                                <title id="c15-title">Metallic value comparison labels</title>
                                <desc id="c15-desc">Labels over three red spheres. The left sphere is marked Metallic 0.0, the center Metallic 0.5 with a warning that it should never be used and a red cross, and the right Metallic 1.0.</desc>
                                <rect x="186" y="214" width="196" height="46" rx="23" fill="#1a1a1f"/>
                                <text x="284" y="245" text-anchor="middle" font-size="22" font-weight="700" fill="#ffffff">Metallic 0.0</text>
                                <rect x="861" y="214" width="196" height="46" rx="23" fill="#1a1a1f"/>
                                <text x="959" y="245" text-anchor="middle" font-size="22" font-weight="700" fill="#ffffff">Metallic 0.5</text>
                                <rect x="1529" y="214" width="196" height="46" rx="23" fill="#1a1a1f"/>
                                <text x="1627" y="245" text-anchor="middle" font-size="22" font-weight="700" fill="#ffffff">Metallic 1.0</text>
                                <rect x="875" y="268" width="168" height="38" rx="19" fill="#E63946"/>
                                <text x="959" y="294" text-anchor="middle" font-size="20" font-weight="700" fill="#ffffff">NEVER USE</text>
                                <g stroke="#E63946" stroke-width="14" stroke-linecap="round" opacity="0.92">
                                <line x1="809" y1="318" x2="1109" y2="618"/>
                                <line x1="1109" y1="318" x2="809" y2="618"/>
                                </g>
                            </svg>
                        </div>
                        <figcaption>Same base color, same roughness, only Metallic changes. 0.0 is a proper non-metal with a colorless highlight; 1.0 is a proper metal with color-tinted reflections and no diffuse. The 0.5 in the middle is physically impossible and always looks off, so Metallic should only ever be 0.0 or 1.0.</figcaption>
                    </figure>
